---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import Yaml from '../components/Yaml.astro';
import customDomain from '../../yaml/custom-domain.yaml?raw';
import oidcProvider from '../../yaml/oidc-provider.yaml?raw';
import enrollRequest from '../../yaml/enroll-request.yaml?raw';

const manifests = [
	{ file: 'custom-domain.yaml', code: customDomain, kind: 'CustomDomain', verb: 'POST', path: '/v1/customdomains', note: 'Serve the API from your own domain, TLS handled for you.' },
	{ file: 'oidc-provider.yaml', code: oidcProvider, kind: 'OIDCProvider', verb: 'POST', path: '/v1/oidcproviders', note: 'Let your own customers sign in with OIDC.' },
	{ file: 'enroll-request.yaml', code: enrollRequest, kind: 'EnrollRequest', verb: 'POST', path: '/v1/enrollrequests', note: 'Even requesting an instance is a resource.' },
];
---
<Layout
	title="ConfigButler: technology & architecture"
	description="How ConfigButler works: a managed Kubernetes-style control plane as the operational truth, with continuous synchronization to a customer-owned Git repository as the durable source of record."
>
	<!-- HERO -->
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="hero-bg"></div>
		<div class="wrap" style="max-width:900px">
			<span class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span>technology &amp; architecture</span>
			<h1 class="display" style="max-width:18ch;margin-top:18px">
				Technology and <span class="b">architecture.</span>
			</h1>
			<p class="lede" style="margin-top:22px;max-width:640px">
				The technical corner. Here's how ConfigButler is put together: the split between operational
				truth and durable record, why <code class="ic">kcp</code> matters, and how continuous sync to Git
				works conceptually.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="wrap"><div class="hr"></div></div>

	<!-- OPERATIONAL TRUTH vs RECORD -->
	<section class="sect">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="sect-head center">
				<span class="eyebrow"><span class="b">the core distinction</span></span>
				<h2 class="sect-h">Two layers, <span class="b">two jobs.</span></h2>
				<p class="lede" style="margin-inline:auto">
					Getting this right is the whole point. Don't confuse the live API with the ledger.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="compare">
				<div class="compare-card rv">
					<h3>Operational truth</h3>
					<div class="sub">the managed control plane</div>
					<ul class="ticklist cb">
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> The live, addressable state of your configuration</li>
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> What applications and clients read and write</li>
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> Kubernetes-style declarative API + watches</li>
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> Where validation, auth, and policy run</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="compare-card cb rv">
					<h3>Source of record</h3>
					<div class="sub">your git repository</div>
					<ul class="ticklist cb">
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> The durable ledger, owned by you</li>
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> Continuously synchronized from live state</li>
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> History, review, promotion, portability</li>
						<li><span class="mk"><Icon name="check" /></span> Integrates with your delivery workflows</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="mono center" style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink-3);margin-top:22px;max-width:70ch;margin-inline:auto">
				Applications and users interact with the <strong style="color:var(--ink)">API</strong>, not Git
				directly. Git is kept in lock-step, automatically: it is the record, not the immediate operational path.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM -->
	<section class="sect sect--ink">
		<div class="wrap cols" style="align-items:center">
			<div class="col-copy">
				<span class="eyebrow"><span class="b">high-level architecture</span></span>
				<h2 class="sect-h" style="margin-top:14px">One managed plane, <span class="b">two directions of truth.</span></h2>
				<p class="lede" style="margin-top:18px">
					ConfigButler runs and manages the live configuration API. Clients interact with that API. Every
					change is validated and continuously recorded to Git, which becomes the durable ledger you own.
					The whole system is designed for portability, auditability, and interoperability.
				</p>
				<div class="badges" style="margin-top:24px">
					<span class="pill">multi-tenant isolation</span>
					<span class="pill">watch semantics</span>
					<span class="pill">schema-first</span>
					<span class="pill">git-synced</span>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="col-visual">
				<div class="term rv">
					<div class="term-bar"><span class="tl"></span><span class="tl"></span><span class="tl"></span><span class="tname">architecture · <span class="b">flow</span></span></div>
					<div style="padding:22px 20px;display:grid;gap:10px">
						<div class="pipe-node" style="background:var(--term-2);border-color:var(--line-dark)"><span class="pi" style="color:var(--brass)"><Icon name="users" /></span><div><div class="pt" style="color:var(--paper)">Clients</div><div class="ps" style="color:var(--ink-4)">humans · apps · Terraform · CLI · agents</div></div></div>
						<div class="pipe-arrow" style="color:var(--ink-4)"><Icon name="down" /></div>
						<div class="pipe-node" style="background:rgba(196,163,90,.10);border-color:var(--brass)"><span class="pi" style="color:var(--brass)"><Icon name="api" /></span><div><div class="pt" style="color:var(--paper)">Managed API: the control plane</div><div class="ps" style="color:var(--ink-4)">validation · auth · watches · audit</div></div></div>
						<div class="pipe-arrow" style="color:var(--ink-4)"><Icon name="down" /></div>
						<div class="pipe-node" style="background:var(--term-2);border-color:var(--line-dark)"><span class="pi" style="color:var(--brass)"><Icon name="git" /></span><div><div class="pt" style="color:var(--paper)">Your Git repository</div><div class="ps" style="color:var(--ink-4)">durable record · history · portability</div></div></div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- WHY KCP -->
	<section class="sect">
		<div class="wrap cols">
			<div class="col-copy">
				<span class="eyebrow"><span class="b">why kcp matters</span></span>
				<h2 class="sect-h" style="margin-top:14px">Isolated configuration APIs, <span class="b">provisioned fast.</span></h2>
				<p class="lede" style="margin-top:18px">
					ConfigButler is powered by Kubernetes-inspired API machinery and <code class="ic">kcp</code>-based
					control-plane patterns. That lets us spin up isolated, low-cost configuration APIs quickly, with
					strong semantics for validation, watching, and structured change handling.
				</p>
				<p class="lede" style="margin-top:14px">
					Crucially: you get the semantics of a Kubernetes API surface <em>without</em> running or operating a
					cluster. It's enabling technology, not something you have to think about day to day.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="col-visual">
				<div class="grid grid-2 rv" style="gap:12px">
					<div class="f"><span class="fi"><Icon name="layers" /></span><div class="ft">Workspace isolation</div><div class="fd">Each tenant or environment gets its own isolated API surface.</div></div>
					<div class="f"><span class="fi"><Icon name="schema" /></span><div class="ft">Schema machinery</div><div class="fd">Typed kinds with validation, reused from proven patterns.</div></div>
					<div class="f"><span class="fi"><Icon name="watch" /></span><div class="ft">Watches</div><div class="fd">Realtime streams of change, not polling.</div></div>
					<div class="f"><span class="fi"><Icon name="rocket" /></span><div class="ft">Fast provisioning</div><div class="fd">Low-cost to stand up a new configuration API.</div></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- MANIFEST OVERVIEW -->
	<section class="sect sect--ink" id="manifests">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="sect-head center">
				<span class="eyebrow"><span class="b">the manifests</span> · everything is a resource</span>
				<h2 class="sect-h">Even the platform is <span class="b">declarative.</span></h2>
				<p class="lede" style="margin-inline:auto">
					Custom domains, OIDC for your customers, and even requesting an instance are themselves typed
					resources. The same KRM you see across this site, collected in the
					<code class="ic">yaml/</code> folder, rendered straight from source.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="manifest-grid">
				{manifests.map((m) => (
					<div class="manifest rv">
						<div class="term">
							<div class="term-bar">
								<span class="tl"></span><span class="tl"></span><span class="tl"></span>
								<span class="tname"><span class="b">{m.kind}</span></span>
								<span class="tright">{m.file}</span>
							</div>
							<div class="reqline"><span class="verb post">{m.verb}</span><span class="path"><span class="b">{m.path}</span></span></div>
							<Yaml code={m.code} />
						</div>
						<p class="manifest-note mono">{m.note}</p>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- CONTINUOUS SYNC -->
	<section class="sect sect--paper-2">
		<div class="wrap cols reverse">
			<div class="col-copy">
				<span class="eyebrow"><span class="b">continuous sync to git</span> · conceptually</span>
				<h2 class="sect-h" style="margin-top:14px">How live state becomes <span class="b">durable record.</span></h2>
				<div class="flow" style="margin-top:24px">
					<div class="flow-step"><span class="n mono">01</span><span class="si"><Icon name="check" /></span><div><div class="st">Change accepted</div><div class="sd">A write passes schema and policy on the live API.</div></div></div>
					<div class="flow-step"><span class="n mono">02</span><span class="si"><Icon name="server" /></span><div><div class="st">Applied to the plane</div><div class="sd">The control plane reflects the new desired state.</div></div></div>
					<div class="flow-step"><span class="n mono">03</span><span class="si"><Icon name="git" /></span><div><div class="st">Reconciled into Git</div><div class="sd">The change is rendered as a clean commit or PR to your repo.</div></div></div>
					<div class="flow-step"><span class="n mono">04</span><span class="si"><Icon name="history" /></span><div><div class="st">Ledger updated</div><div class="sd">Your repo now holds the authoritative, portable history.</div></div></div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="col-visual">
				<div class="card card-pad rv sticky prose">
					<h3 style="margin-top:0">Where open source fits</h3>
					<p style="font-size:15px">
						The reconciliation engine that turns API activity into clean YAML commits is developed in the
						open as <strong>GitOps Reverser</strong>. Streaming resource flows come from <strong>KRM Stream</strong>.
						The wider operating model is <a href="https://reversegitops.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Reverse GitOps</strong></a>.
					</p>
					<a class="link" href="/open-source">Explore the open source <Icon name="arrow" /></a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- CTA -->
	<section class="sect">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="cta-band rv">
				<span class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span>go deeper</span>
				<h2 class="sect-h" style="margin-top:14px;max-width:22ch">Want the detailed architecture?</h2>
				<p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
					This page stays high-level on purpose. If you want to go deep on isolation, watch semantics, or
					the sync engine, the fastest path is a conversation.
				</p>
				<div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:28px">
					<a class="btn btn-brass" href="/about#contact"><span class="d"></span>Talk to Simon</a>
					<a class="btn btn-ghost-dark" href="/open-source">Browse open source →</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
